## 25.12 Selective prediction and abstention

Responsible deployment often requires a model not to answer every query.  A selective classifier returns either a prediction or an abstention symbol $\bot$:

$$
g(x)\in\{0,1,\bot\}.
$$

For a confidence score $c(x)$ and threshold $\tau$, a simple rule is

$$
g_\tau(x)=
\begin{cases}
\widehat{y}(x), & c(x)\ge \tau,\\
\bot, & c(x)<\tau.
\end{cases}
$$

Two quantities are central:

$$
\operatorname{coverage}(\tau)=P(c(X)\ge \tau),
$$

$$
\operatorname{selective\ risk}(\tau)
=E[L(\widehat{Y},Y)\mid c(X)\ge \tau].
$$

Increasing $\tau$ often decreases risk among answered cases but also decreases coverage.

