## 25.21 Chapter summary

Ethics in NLP is not only a philosophical add-on after model training.  It begins when we choose the data distribution, target variable, representation, model class, objective function, metric, and deployment rule.  Mathematics helps because it forces these choices to become explicit.

The core lessons are:

- responsible NLP starts with random variables and distributions;
- bias can enter through data, measurement, representation, optimization, and deployment;
- fairness metrics are conditional probabilities, not universal moral truths;
- different fairness metrics can conflict when base rates differ;
- calibration, uncertainty, and distribution shift are central to deployment;
- privacy and factuality require separate mathematical treatment;
- AI assistance can support auditing, but evidence must come from definitions, data, and transparent evaluation.

